Not only that, but many would regard it as offensive to put Jesus' words
(Matt. 7:7, Luke 11:9) into the mouth of a mythical god in a computer game.

This is on a completely different subject, but I've often wanted to see
spells be a bit more elaborate to cast.

Let's say we have a spellbook of scare monster.  We'll take a text from
a first millennium B.C. Syrian incantation, and go from there:

  You open the spellbook.   --More--
  You read the words, "Bet abo bal tabona."  --More--
  You mispronounce the words.  --More--
  (Monsters suddenly know where you are.)

Probably it's getting too elaborate, but another twist could be.

  You open the spellbook.   --More--
  You read the words, "Bet abo bal tabo!"  --More--
  You mispronounce one of the words.  --More--
  You have summoned the god Bel.  --More--

If you switch e for a in "bal" you get Bel, the name by which
Palestinians and Syrians in this era knew the great Mesopotamian
deity, Marduk.  The quote is modified from an early first millen-
nium BC Syrian incantation.

  Bel thunders, "You dare to toy with the gods?  (something bad occurs)"

There are all kinds of scenarios that could be worked up in this
manner using real spells in Hebrew, Akkadian, Phoenician, Greek,
Egyptian, Latin, Aramaic, and other such ancient languages.

It would just give everything more of a fun spirit if the incantations
were real, and the mistakes had some more logical consequences.

